  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1129 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nWe remain within a moist, active pattern across Southern NM and \nFar W. TX with a subtropical tap on southerly flow. The \nsubtropical ridge, sprawled over the Southern Plains and SE CONUS,\nis driving this flow. Within the southerly flow, WV imagery is \nshowing a weak disturbance/MCV over Sonora with speed diffluence \nover NM and AZ. One wave is exiting our CWA now, dragging the bulk\nof our precip with it for the moment, but the moist environment \n(PW values 1.1\" - 1.4\"), diffluence aloft, and approaching UV \nwave is keeping some showers on the radar across southern NM and \nNorthern Chihuahua. We will see a lull this late morning, but by \nearly afternoon and especially into the evening overnight hours, \nguidance shows more precip developing/moving in, especially along \nand west of the Rio Grande. The current Flash Flood Watch looks \ngood, but we may need extensions (see next paragraph).\n\nThe wave over Sonora will lift out tonight into tomorrow morning,\nbut we will remain within our moist tap through Saturday. This \ncombined with the passage of weak disturbances will keep precip \nchances alive through then with more rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms expected. The Flash Flood Watch expires at 6AM \nThursday, so we will assess the need for any extensions in both \ntime and area. A deeper trough will sweep through Saturday, and it\nwill push our moisture east. The devil is in the details though \nbecause subsequent thunderstorm development will depend on what \nhappens the day before, so although rain chances prevail through \nSaturday, confidence on individual details is low. Rain chances \nwill end with the passage of the trough, so Sunday will be a \nquieter today as will Monday and Tuesday. We will also see \nincreasing influence of the UL ridge, further suppressing \nconvection. Our break ends on Wednesday with the return of \nmoisture and increasing influence of a closed low to our west, but\nthat is just beyond the forecast window.",
